pkcs7 is the padding, not the cipher

Every function in this file was a stub that decrypted nothing and answered 8, so a caller got
eight bytes of whatever was already in its output buffer. iA Writer keeps its Markdown regular
expressions in an encrypted resource and decrypts them at startup, so the expressions were nil
strings and the failure surfaced as

    NSInvalidArgumentException: Nil string passed to NSRegularExpression API

from inside a dispatch_once, with nothing to say that a cipher was involved.

Decrypting is the whole of it: run the blocks through the mode, then read the last byte of the
plaintext as the number of bytes that were added and drop them. A CORRUPT PAD IS NOT A CRASH:
if the last byte is not a length this cipher could have written, the whole plaintext is
returned rather than a negative length, because the caller decides what a bad decryption means
and an underflow here would be a very long memcpy somewhere else.

